ABSTRACT
With the rapid development of globalization and technological progress, modern
engineering projects have become increasingly complex. In this context, construction risk
management has become particularly crucial. Projects face various potential risks, such
as geological, construction process, and equipment issues. Ensuring the safety of the
construction process is not only a key factor for project success but also involves the
safety of participants' lives and the sustainability of the social environment. Therefore,
this paper conducts in-depth research on the safety construction risks during the shield
tunneling phase of a specific metro project to provide scientific support for engineering
projects, addressing various risks that may occur in the complex construction
environment and meeting the modern engineering projects' demands for safety, reliability,
and sustainability.
Firstly, construction risk identification and assessment are crucial steps, involving
the application of advanced technologies and methods to systematically identify and
understand potential construction risks, providing a foundation for implementing
corresponding measures. The formulation of construction risk control strategies aims to
minimize adverse impacts, including preventive measures, improvements in construction
processes, and increased equipment reliability, thus reducing the likelihood of accidents.
Timely monitoring of construction risks is essential for identifying and addressing new
risks, ensuring the continuous capability of construction risk management. Secondly, by
outlining the fundamental principles, engineering processes, and key technologies of
shield tunneling, this paper provides background knowledge for understanding and
analyzing the roots and impacts of construction risks. Introducing the Qualitative
Comparative Analysis (QCA) method as an assessment tool for complex systems reveals
intricate correlations among different construction risk factors, aiding in a more accurate
evaluation of the impact and probability of construction risks. Lastly, the evaluation of 15


